  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1129 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nA stationary boundary remains draped over portions of the Mid-\nSouth through the first half of today, with scattered clouds\ngenerally south of the I-40 corridor. The location of this\nboundary has allowed much of the Mid-South to remain on the\n\"cooler\" side for mid July, with some locations across north to\ncentral MS still approaching 90 by this afternoon. Some scattered\nshowers have developed to the east of the area, with isolated\nchances this afternoon across NE MS. With the isolated shower\nchances this afternoon, calm winds, and generally low dew point\ndepressions overnight, some patchy fog may develop for locations\nsouth of the stalled boundary into Friday morning.\n\nFor Friday, the aforementioned boundary will begin to lift back\nnorthward through the daytime, with isolated to scattered chances\nfor showers and thunderstorms generally along and south of I-40.\nBy Friday night into Saturday, a weak MCS will descend out of\nMissouri and move across the Mid-South. With the timing of this\nsystem, much of the CAM guidance that is within range shows some\npotential for elevated CAPE, but generally weak lapse rates.\nShear could increase very locally as the MCS approaches, but for\nthe most part remains too low for organized severe weather. Will\nnot entirely rule out a strong thunderstorm or two, but overall\nthe threat appears to be very low at this time. Any thunderstorm\nactivity along this system will move out through the day on\nSaturday, leaving behind dry conditions by Sunday. As far as\ntemperatures go over the next few days, we will remain near to\njust above normal through Friday with high temperatures spanning\nthe 80s to low 90s. By Saturday, high temperatures will begin to\ncreep back into the low 90s for much of the area. A Heat Advisory\nmay be needed for locations west of the MS River and across the\nMS Delta on Saturday, but there is enough uncertainty with the\naforementioned system that confidence is still low.\n\nLREF and NBM guidance remains in generally good agreement for a\ndry and increasingly hot forecast by Sunday into at least the\nmiddle of next week. An upper-level ridge will continue to build\nacross the Rockies into the Central Plains, with the Mid-South\neither right on the cusp on the ridge or well beneath. This will\nallow a much more hot and humid airmass to return to the region,\nkeeping high temperatures in the mid to upper 90s Sunday through\nat least next Wednesday. With dew point values increasing back to\nthe low to mid 70s, this will allow daily heat indices of at\nleast 105, many potentially reaching over 110. As such, heat\nheadlines will likely be needed by at least Sunday, continuing\ninto next week.",
